": "call-16431f54-2600-4652-b8fe-1ac76e448d9f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "evidence": "Workspace inspection confirmed /workspace contains only task.json (511 bytes) restating the question; no SiT code, Hyena code, paper PDF, dataset, or ML frameworks present. Environment: Python 3.14.4, 224 CPUs, 2 TB RAM, no GPU, no network. Researcher-topic pointers (neurips2024/93022/6:setup_4, setup_5) identify the source paper but its content is not in the workspace. Hyena properties (implicit long convolution, factorised multi-scale kernels, O(T log T) FFT-based computation, causal vs. non-causal variants) are attributed to Poli et al. 2023 as prior knowledge. The design specifies two fixed conditions (setup_4: SiT+time attention, setup_5: SiT+non-causal Hyena) with optional causal-Hyena and extended-context ablations, a pre-registered primary metric (length-before-collapse on normalised metric drift), secondary metrics (spectral entropy, band-power ratio, sliding-window FID, autocorrelation decay, FLOPs), paired statistical testing (Wilcoxon signed-rank on T_collapse, Mann-Whitney U on spectral metrics with Bonferroni correction), explicit controls (dataset, model size, training recipe, seeds, hardware), and a GPU-cluster feasibility estimate (2-3 weeks core on 4x A100).", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Non-Causal Hyena Operator for Long-Horizon Trajectory Generation in SiT\n\n## 1. Rationale & Hypothesis\n\n### 1.1 Motivation\n\nThe SiT architecture (NeurIPS 2024, #93022) employs a dedicated time-attention sub-layer to model temporal dependencies within generated trajectories. Standard multi-head self-attention over the temporal axis has O(T\u00b2) computational and memory complexity in sequence length T, and its learned attention weights, while expressive, provide no inductive bias toward the multi-scale temporal structure that characterises physical and natural dynamics.\n\nThe Hyena operator (Poli et al., 2023) replaces attention with an implicit long convolution whose kernel is factorised across multiple scales: K(\u03c4) = \u220f_{s=1}^{S} k_s(\u03c4), where each factor k_s is a smaller kernel operating at a different effective receptive-field scale. This factorisation is computed via FFT in O(T log T) time and O(T) memory, and inherently captures dependencies at multiple timescales simultaneously\u2014fast dynamics via narrow kernels, slow drifts via the composite product of wide kernels. Critically, Hyena can be instantiated in a **non-causal (bidirectional)** mode, where each position attends to both past and future context, which is natural for trajectory generation paradigms that produce the full sequence (diffusion, flow-matching, or one-shot denoising) rather than token-by-token.\n\n### 1.2 Formal Hypotheses\n\n- **H\u2081 (Primary):** Replacing the time-attention sub-layer in SiT with a non-causal Hyena operator (setup_5) enables generation of trajectories that are (a) longer before quality collapse, (b) more internally consistent at long horizons, and (c) exhibit richer multi-timescale spectral structure, relative to SiT with its original time attention (setup_4).\n\n- **H\u2082 (Mechanism / Ablation):** The improvement under H\u2081 is driven substantially by the *non-causal (bidirectional)* receptive field of the Hyena operator, not merely by the convolutional kernel structure. A causal Hyena variant should show a smaller or null gain over the attention baseline at very long horizons.\n\n- **H\u2080 (Null):** The non-causal Hyena replacement does not improve long-horizon consistency or multi-timescale spectral coverage beyond what the original time attention achieves at matched model size and compute.\n\n### 1.3 Theoretical Grounding\n\nThe expected advantage rests on three properties of the Hyena operator (Poli et al., 2023):\n1. **Multi-scale factorised kernels** provide an inductive bias toward dynamics at multiple temporal frequencies, directly addressing the \"multiple timescales\" requirement.\n2. **O(T log T) complexity** (via FFT-based implicit convolution) removes the O(T\u00b2) bottleneck of attention, making generation at very long T computationally feasible.\n3. **Non-causal (bidirectional) context** allows each temporal position to integrate information from both directions, which is critical for trajectory consistency where local dynamics must be coherent with global structure.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design & Conditions\n\n### 2.1 Core (Fixed) Conditions\n\n| ID | Condition | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | SiT + Time Attention (setup_4) | The original SiT architecture with its native time-attention sub-layer. This is the baseline. All non-temporal sub-layers (spatial/channel mixing, MLP, normalisation, embeddings) are held at the paper's defaults. |\n| **B** | SiT + Non-Causal Hyena (setup_5) | SiT with the time-attention sub-layer replaced by a non-causal Hyena operator (see \u00a72.3 for architectural spec). All other sub-layers identical to A. |\n\n### 2.2 Optional Secondary Ablations\n\nThese are included for mechanistic insight but are **not required** to answer the primary question. They should be run only if compute budget permits.\n\n| ID | Condition | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| **C** | SiT + Causal Hyena | Same Hyena operator as B but with a causal (one-sided) mask. Tests H\u2082: isolates the contribution of bidirectionality. |\n| **A\u2032** | SiT + Time Attention (extended context) | The original time attention in A but with a larger context window (e.g., 2\u00d7 or 4\u00d7 the default window size, or full global attention if the default is windowed). Controls for the possibility that any long-range model benefits simply from more context, not from the specific operator. |\n\n### 2.3 Architectural Specification: The Hyena Replacement\n\n**What is swapped:** The single time-attention sub-layer within each SiT block. In a typical SiT block, the forward pass is:\n\n```\nx \u2192 [Time-Attention] \u2192 Add & Norm \u2192 [Spatial/Channel Mixing] \u2192 Add & Norm \u2192 [FFN] \u2192 Add & Norm \u2192 x'\n```\n\nIn condition B, the `[Time-Attention]` module is replaced by a `[Non-Causal Hyena]` module. The input and output dimensions (embedding dim d, sequence length T) are unchanged. The residual connection and LayerNorm around the block are preserved.\n\n**Hyena operator parameterisation:**\n\n- **Number of factorised scales S:** 4 (matching the default in Poli et al., 2023 for medium-scale models). Each scale s has an effective kernel width w_s = w_max / 2^s, where w_max is the maximum kernel width (e.g., w_max = 2T or w_max = 4096, whichever is smaller).\n- **Kernel factorisation:** The full implicit kernel K(\u03c4) = k_1(\u03c4) \u00b7 k_2(\u03c4) \u00b7 \u2026 \u00b7 k_S(\u03c4), where each k_s is a learned 1D kernel of width w_s. This is the \"implicit\" formulation: the full kernel is never materialised; instead, the convolution is computed by applying S sequential FFT-based convolutions, each with kernel k_s.\n- **Causality:** Non-causal (bidirectional). Each k_s is a symmetric (or freely parameterised, non-causal) kernel. The FFT convolution uses zero-padding and no causal masking.\n- **FFT-based computation:** Each factorised convolution is computed as: y_s = IFFT(FFT(x_padded) \u00b7 FFT(k_s_padded)), where padding ensures linear (non-circular) convolution. The S convolutions are applied sequentially: x \u2192 conv(k_1) \u2192 conv(k_2) \u2192 \u2026 \u2192 conv(k_S) \u2192 output.\n- **Input / output projection:** A linear projection W_in \u2208 \u211d^{d\u00d7d} before the first kernel and W_out \u2208 \u211d^{d\u00d7d} after the last, matching the parameter count of the attention layer (Q, K, V, O projections).\n- **Total parameter count:** Matched to the attention layer's parameter count (4d\u00b2 for QKVO projections + S \u00d7 w_s \u00d7 d for kernel parameters). If the kernel parameters are a small fraction, the projection dimensions are adjusted to maintain parity.\n\n### 2.4 Generation Horizon Sweep\n\nAll conditions generate trajectories at T \u2208 {T_train, 2\u00b7T_train, 4\u00b7T_train, 8\u00b7T_train, 16\u00b7T_train}, where T_train is the sequence length used during training (to be confirmed from the paper; if unavailable, assume T_train = 512 and sweep {512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192}). The longest T is chosen to be the maximum the architecture supports without OOM on the target hardware.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Metrics & Analysis Plan\n\n### 3.1 Primary Outcome (Pre-Registered)\n\n**Primary metric: Length-before-collapse (T_collapse)**, defined as the longest generation horizon T at which the *normalised metric drift* remains within a pre-registered threshold.\n\n- **Metric drift at horizon T:** For a generated trajectory {z_1, \u2026, z_T}, compute the mean pairwise distance in latent space within a sliding window of width W = T_train: drift(T) = (1/(T\u2212W)) \u03a3_{t=1}^{T\u2212W} \u2016z_{t+W/2} \u2212 z_t\u2016\u2082 / \u03c3_data, where \u03c3_data is the per-dimension standard deviation of ground-truth trajectories.\n- **Threshold:** T_collapse is the largest T in the sweep where drift(T) \u2264 2 \u00d7 drift(T_train). (The factor of 2 is pre-registered; sensitivity analysis at 1.5\u00d7 and 3\u00d7 is reported as secondary.)\n- **Why this metric:** It directly operationalises \"extremely long and consistent.\" A model that generates longer trajectories while maintaining local consistency (low drift) has a higher T_collapse.\n\n### 3.2 Secondary Metrics\n\n| Metric | Definition | What it tests |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Spectral entropy** at T = 8\u00b7T_train | Shannon entropy H = \u2212\u03a3 p_f log p_f of the normalised power spectral density (PSD) of the generated trajectory (averaged over channels and samples). | Multi-timescale coverage: higher H = more frequency bands populated. |\n| **Band-power ratio** | Ratio of PSD energy in low (0\u20130.1 Nyquist), mid (0.1\u20130.5), and high (0.5\u20131.0) frequency bands, compared to the dataset's ratio. | Whether specific timescales are under/over-represented. |\n| **Sliding-window FID / FVD** | FID (or FVD for video) computed on overlapping windows of width W = T_train, sliding with step W/2 along the full trajectory. Mean \u00b1 std across windows. | Local perceptual quality stability at long horizons. |\n| **Autocorrelation decay length** | The lag \u03c4 at which the trajectory's autocorrelation drops below 1/e. | Effective range of temporal dependencies captured. |\n| **Inference FLOPs & peak memory** | Measured at each T for each condition. | Computational cost of the long-horizon generation. |\n\n### 3.3 Statistical Analysis Plan\n\n1. **Primary test:** For each seed, compute T_collapse(A) and T_collapse(B). With K \u2265 3 seeds per condition, perform a **paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test** (or paired t-test if normality holds) on the T_collapse values. Report the median difference, 95% bootstrap CI (10,000 resamples), and effect size (rank-biserial correlation r_RB).\n   - Rationale for non-parametric: T_collapse is ordinal (it takes values from the discrete sweep), and K may be small (3\u20135).\n\n2. **Secondary tests:** For spectral entropy and band-power ratios at each T, use a **two-sample Mann-Whitney U test** (A vs. B) with Bonferroni correction across the T values. Report medians and IQRs.\n\n3. **Ablation read-out (if C and A\u2032 are run):** Three-way comparison A vs. B vs. C using Kruskal-Wallis test, with Dunn's post-hoc pairwise tests. If A\u2032 is run, compare A\u2032 vs. B to isolate architecture from context length.\n\n4. **Paired generation:** All conditions use the **same noise seeds** (for diffusion/flow-matching) or the **same initial conditions** (for autoregressive) at each T, enabling paired comparisons that reduce variance.\n\n5. **Pre-registration:** The primary metric, threshold, sweep values, and statistical test are fixed before any model is trained. No metric is selected post-hoc.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls\n\nThe following are held **identical** across all conditions (A, B, and optional C, A\u2032):\n\n| Control | Specification |\n|---|---|\n| **Dataset** | Same trajectory dataset from the paper. Same train/val/test split. No data augmentation differences. |\n| **Model size** | Same total parameter count. Non-temporal sub-layers (spatial/channel mixing, FFN, embeddings, output head) are architecturally identical and receive the same initialisation. |\n| **Training recipe** | Same optimiser (e.g., AdamW), learning rate schedule, batch size, number of training steps, gradient clipping, and weight decay. |\n| **Non-temporal layer initialisation** | Shared initialisation (same random seed) for all non-temporal parameters across conditions, so that only the temporal operator differs at step 0. |\n| **Random seeds** | K \u2265 3 independent training seeds per condition. For generation, the same K noise/initial-condition seeds are used across all conditions at each T. |\n| **Evaluation protocol** | Same held-out test set, same number of generated trajectories per test sample (e.g., 5), same sampling/denoising steps. |\n| **Hardware & software** | Same GPU model, same framework version, same precision (fp16/bf16). |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Feasibility Assessment\n\n### 5.1 Compute Requirements (Qualitative Estimate)\n\nThis design is a **protocol for a standard GPU cluster**, not constrained by the current CPU-only sandbox.\n\n| Phase | Estimate |\n|---|---|\n| **Training (per condition, per seed)** | SiT at small/medium scale (e.g., ~100M\u2013500M params) on a trajectory dataset: ~2\u20135 days on 4\u00d7 A100-80GB or 2\u00d7 H100, depending on T_train and dataset size. The Hyena condition (B) should be *faster* per step than attention (A) at long T due to O(T log T) vs. O(T\u00b2), but similar at T_train if T_train is short. |\n| **Generation sweep** | Negligible compared to training. Even at T = 8192, a single trajectory takes seconds to minutes on one A100. 5 trajectories \u00d7 5 T values \u00d7 3 seeds \u00d7 3 conditions \u2248 a few hours. |\n| **Total (core: A + B, 3 seeds)** | ~2\u20133 weeks wall-clock on 4\u00d7 A100-80GB, including training, generation, and metric computation. |\n| **Total (with ablations: A + B + C + A\u2032, 3 seeds)** | ~4\u20135 weeks on the same hardware. |\n\n### 5.2 Minimal Viable Configuration\n\nIf compute is limited, the design degrades gracefully:\n- Reduce to K = 2 seeds (less statistical power but still informative).\n- Reduce the T sweep to {T_train, 4\u00b7T_train, 8\u00b7T_train}.\n- Drop ablations C and A\u2032.\n- Use a smaller SiT variant (e.g., 50M params) \u2014 the architectural comparison is the point, not scale.\n\n### 5.3 Implementation Notes\n\n- **Hyena implementation:** The non-causal Hyena layer can be implemented using a standard FFT-based 1D convolution (e.g., `torch.fft.rfft` / `torch.fft.irfft` for real signals) applied sequentially for each scale factor. No custom CUDA kernels are required for a first implementation; the FFT path is sufficient at the sequence lengths considered.\n- **Parameter matching:** After implementing the Hyena layer, verify that the total parameter count of condition B matches condition A within \u00b12%. If the kernel parameters are negligible, increase the projection dimension d_proj slightly.\n- **Evaluation code:** The metric drift, PSD, and spectral entropy computations are straightforward numpy/scipy operations and can be run on CPU post-generation.\n\n### 5.4 Risks & Mitigations\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Hyena layer is harder to train than attention (slower convergence) | Monitor training curves; if B lags significantly at T_train, report it. The hypothesis is about *long-horizon* performance, not short-horizon training speed. If needed, allow B up to 1.5\u00d7 training steps. |\n| The time-attention in SiT is not a simple full-attention but has a specific structure (e.g., dilated, factored) not captured by \"standard attention\" | The design specifies \"the paper's time-attention sub-layer\" as the unit of replacement. The exact internal structure is an implementation detail that does not change the experimental logic. |\n| \"Non-causal\" Hyena may overfit to bidirectional patterns that don't transfer to the generation process | If the generation is autoregressive (not one-shot), the non-causal Hyena would need to be applied in a chunked or denoising context. The design assumes a one-shot / diffusion generation paradigm (see Assumption A3). If the paper uses autoregressive generation, the Hyena would be applied within each denoising step or chunk, and the causal ablation (C) becomes more central. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Assumptions\n\nThe following assumptions are stated explicitly. The design is robust to reasonable variations of each; none blocks execution.\n\n| # | Assumption | Justification / Robustness |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A1** | \"Time attention\" in SiT is a dedicated sub-layer within each transformer block that operates over the temporal axis, distinct from spatial/channel mixing. | Standard in spatio-temporal transformer architectures. If SiT uses a unified attention over (time \u00d7 space) tokens, the replacement would be a temporal-only Hyena applied to the time dimension of the attention output; the experimental logic is unchanged. |\n| **A2** | \"Non-causal Hyena\" refers to the bidirectional implicit long convolution from Poli et al. (2023), not a modified variant specific to the paper. | If the paper uses a modified Hyena (e.g., with learned scale selection or a different factorisation), the parameterisation in \u00a72.3 should be updated to match, but the conditions, metrics, and analysis plan remain identical. |\n| **A3** | The generation paradigm is one-shot or iterative-denoising (diffusion / flow-matching), producing the full trajectory sequence, not strictly autoregressive token-by-token generation. | This is the natural setting for \"non-causal\" to be meaningful. If the paper uses autoregressive generation, the Hyena is applied within each conditional step, and the causal ablation (C) becomes the primary contrast rather than a secondary one. The metric suite is unchanged. |\n| **A4** | \"Multiple timescales\" is operationalised as the presence of dynamics at different temporal frequencies, measurable via PSD / wavelet decomposition. | This is the most falsifiable interpretation. An alternative (the model handles both local and global dependencies) is partially captured by the autocorrelation decay length metric. |\n| **A5** | \"Extremely long\" means 4\u00d7\u201316\u00d7 the training sequence length. | The sweep in \u00a72.4 covers this range. If the paper has a specific target length, the sweep should be centred on it. |\n| **A6** | The paper provides a training recipe (dataset, hyper-parameters, model size) sufficient to reproduce the baseline SiT. | If a pretrained checkpoint is available, conditions A and B can be initialised from it (with the temporal sub-layer re-initialised for B), reducing training cost. If not, training from scratch is required. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Summary of What Is Not Known (and Does Not Block the Design)\n\n- The exact internal structure of the SiT time-attention layer (full vs. windowed vs. factored). \u2192 \u00a72.3 specifies the replacement at the sub-layer level; internal details are implementation specifics.\n- The trajectory dataset name, size, and modality. \u2192 The metric suite includes modality-agnostic metrics (drift, PSD, spectral entropy) and modality-specific ones (FVD for video) that can be selected once the dataset is confirmed.\n- The exact training sequence length T_train. \u2192 The sweep is parameterised in terms of T_train; concrete values are filled in at implementation time.\n- Whether the paper already contains a Hyena ablation in setup_4/setup_5. \u2192 If it does, this design extends it with the multi-timescale spectral analysis and the 